torch::Tensor parallel_scan_cuda(torch::Tensor x, int dim) {
    auto sizes = x.sizes();
    int n = sizes[dim];
    int total_elements = x.numel();
    int outer_elements = total_elements / n;

    auto x_reshaped = x.reshape({outer_elements, n}).contiguous();
    auto out = torch::zeros_like(x_reshaped);

    auto x_ptr = x_reshaped.data_ptr<float>();
    auto out_ptr = out.data_ptr<float>();

    int threads = 256;
    int shared_mem_size = n * sizeof(float);

    row_scan_kernel<float><<<outer_elements, threads, shared_mem_size>>>(
        x_ptr, out_ptr, outer_elements, n
    );
```

The function reshapes input, allocates output, configures kernel parameters, and launches only `row_scan_kernel`. The `inclusive_scan_kernel` remains completely unused throughout execution.

For the [128, 4000] input with dim=1, this means 128 rows of 4000 elements each get processed by `row_scan_kernel`, which performs sequential scanning where thread 0 handles all computation.

The kernel implements an inclusive prefix sum - each element becomes the cumulative sum up to that point, matching `torch.cumsum(x, dim=1)` behavior. Since `inclusive_scan_kernel` is never actually invoked during execution, only defined in the code, the mutation there has no effect on the output. The mutant remains unkillable because the altered function path simply doesn't execute.
